q('Grip release is delayed after a forceful handshake; examination shows distal weakness and the patient has early cataracts. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1',[
 ('Typical inflammatory proximal myositis','Myotonia and early cataracts suggest a multisystem inherited disorder.'),('A pure median entrapment neuropathy','This does not explain myotonia and systemic findings.'),('Lambert–Eaton syndrome','Facilitation and autonomic features differ from delayed relaxation.')],
 'Myotonia is delayed relaxation, not fatigability', '**DM1 combines myotonia with muscle and multisystem disease.**', ['Facial and distal weakness can be prominent.','Cataracts, endocrine and cardiac manifestations are important.'], '**Assess beyond the presenting hand symptom.**', ['Cardiac conduction and respiratory disease can threaten life.','Offer genetic counseling and family evaluation.'], [['Myotonia','Delayed relaxation'],['DM1 pattern','Often distal/facial'],['Systemic review','Heart, breathing, eyes and endocrine']], [DM], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which genetic lesion causes myotonic dystrophy type 1?', 'CTG repeat expansion in DMPK',[
 ('CAG expansion in HTT','This causes Huntington disease.'),('GAA expansion in FXN','This causes Friedreich ataxia.'),('PMP22 duplication','This commonly causes CMT1A.')],
 'Repeat identity determines the genetic diagnosis', '**DM1 is an autosomal dominant repeat-expansion disorder.**', ['Anticipation can occur.','Congenital severe disease is particularly associated with maternal transmission.'], '**Counsel using the family and molecular context.**', ['Repeat size does not yield a perfectly precise individual prognosis.','Discuss reproductive and cascade-testing implications.'], [['DM1','DMPK CTG'],['HD','HTT CAG'],['Friedreich','FXN GAA']], [DM])
q('Why does a patient with myotonic dystrophy need cardiac surveillance even with modest limb weakness?', 'Conduction disease and arrhythmia can occur independently of the apparent skeletal muscle severity',[
 ('Cardiac involvement occurs only after complete loss of walking','That would delay detection.'),('A normal limb CK permanently excludes heart disease','CK is not a conduction surveillance test.'),('Myotonia treatment eliminates every arrhythmic risk','Cardiac assessment remains necessary.')],
 'A multisystem myopathy requires multisystem monitoring', '**DM1 can cause serious conduction and rhythm disturbances.**', ['ECG and further surveillance depend on findings and specialist guidance.','Symptoms such as syncope need prompt assessment.'], '**Review respiratory and anesthetic risks too.**', ['Sleep-related hypoventilation can be important.','Coordinate procedures with an informed team.'], [['Limb severity','Does not fully predict cardiac risk'],['Surveillance','Cardiac assessment'],['Syncope','Urgent review']], [DM])
q('A boy has early progressive proximal weakness, Gowers maneuver, calf enlargement and very high CK. Which disorder is most likely?', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy',[
 ('Typical adult IBM','The age and weakness pattern are incompatible.'),('An isolated motor endplate disorder','The dystrophic progression and calf pseudohypertrophy favor muscle degeneration.'),('A sensory axonal neuropathy','Sensory loss is not the central syndrome.')],
 'A dystrophinopathy can present with delayed motor performance', '**DMD is usually an X-linked disorder with severe dystrophin deficiency.**', ['Calf enlargement reflects pseudohypertrophy.','Gowers maneuver reflects proximal weakness rather than a disease-specific sign alone.'], '**Confirm genetically and assess heart and lungs.**', ['Current disease-directed options are mutation-, age- and jurisdiction-specific.','Multidisciplinary care and carrier counseling remain central.'], [['Weakness','Early proximal'],['Protein','Severe dystrophin deficiency'],['Care','Genetic, cardiac and respiratory']], [DU], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What generally distinguishes Becker from Duchenne muscular dystrophy?', 'Later or milder skeletal muscle disease with partially functional dystrophin in many cases',[
 ('Becker never involves the heart','Cardiomyopathy can be clinically important.'),('Becker is an acquired autoimmune disorder','It is part of the inherited dystrophinopathy spectrum.'),('Normal walking ability excludes every dystrophinopathy','Mild and cardiac-predominant phenotypes occur.')],
 'Dystrophin function influences a clinical spectrum', '**In-frame variants often produce a Becker phenotype, but exceptions occur.**', ['Duchenne more often has severe protein disruption.','Genotype-phenotype interpretation is not absolute.'], '**Do not under-monitor the heart in mild skeletal disease.**', ['Offer appropriate family and carrier assessment.','Use genetic confirmation rather than the age label alone.'], [['Duchenne','Usually severe early muscle disease'],['Becker','Often partial protein function'],['Both','Cardiac surveillance needed']], [DU])
q('An adult has limb-girdle weakness with disproportionately early diaphragmatic weakness and a large fall in supine vital capacity. Which treatable metabolic myopathy should be considered?', 'Late-onset Pompe disease',[
 ('McArdle disease as the most characteristic respiratory phenotype','McArdle more typically presents with exertional cramps and second wind.'),('An isolated common fibular neuropathy','This cannot explain diaphragmatic and girdle weakness.'),('Typical ocular MG proven by the vital-capacity pattern alone','The broader myopathic phenotype needs evaluation.')],
 'Respiratory weakness can precede advanced limb disability', '**Pompe disease results from acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency with lysosomal glycogen accumulation.**', ['Late-onset disease often affects axial and respiratory muscles.','Infantile disease has a different prominent cardiomyopathic phenotype.'], '**Confirm with enzyme and molecular testing.**', ['Disease-directed enzyme therapy and respiratory support can change care.','Assess nocturnal hypoventilation rather than relying only on daytime symptoms.'], [['Enzyme','Acid alpha-glucosidase'],['Adult clue','Girdle/axial plus respiratory weakness'],['Action','Confirm and treat through specialist care']], [PO], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Exercise causes painful cramps early, but symptoms improve after slowing briefly and continuing moderate activity: the second-wind phenomenon. Which disease is favored?', 'McArdle disease',[
 ('CPT II deficiency as the classic second-wind disorder','CPT II attacks more often follow prolonged exercise or fasting.'),('Myasthenia gravis','This is fatigable transmission failure rather than an energy-substrate phenotype.'),('Typical hypokalemic periodic paralysis','Episodic flaccid weakness with low potassium is a different presentation.')],
 'Trigger timing identifies a metabolic pathway', '**McArdle disease is muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency.**', ['Early exercise depends heavily on muscle glycogen availability.','A switch toward circulating fuels helps explain second wind.'], '**Prevent rhabdomyolysis through an individualized activity plan.**', ['Avoid forcing activity through severe pain or contracture.','Genetic confirmation and specialist exercise advice are useful.'], [['Early intense activity','Often symptomatic'],['Second wind','Characteristic clue'],['Defect','Muscle glycogen phosphorylase']], [MC], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Recurrent rhabdomyolysis follows prolonged exercise during fasting, with normal strength between attacks. Which metabolic defect is especially relevant?', 'Myopathic CPT II deficiency',[
 ('DMPK expansion','This causes a chronic multisystem myotonic disorder.'),('PMP22 deletion','This causes pressure-sensitive neuropathy.'),('AChR autoantibodies','These do not explain the characteristic recurrent muscle breakdown.')],
 'Fatty-acid use becomes important during sustained energy demand', '**CPT II deficiency can impair long-chain fatty-acid oxidation.**', ['Prolonged exercise, fasting, cold and illness can trigger attacks.','Interictal examination can be normal.'], '**Use an attack-prevention and emergency nutrition plan.**', ['Avoid prolonged fasting and unsafe exertion.','Confirm with appropriate biochemical and molecular testing.'], [['Trigger','Fasting/prolonged exercise'],['Between attacks','Can be normal'],['Pathway','Long-chain fatty-acid metabolism']], [CP], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Recurrent flaccid limb weakness follows rest after exercise or a large carbohydrate meal; potassium is low during an attack. Which syndrome is most consistent?', 'Hypokalemic periodic paralysis',[
 ('A sensory ganglionopathy','The episodic motor syndrome with low potassium does not fit.'),('Typical chronic inflammatory myopathy','Attacks with recovery and electrolyte shifts suggest another mechanism.'),('A fixed spinal compression as the best explanation','Compression does not usually produce this trigger-associated recurring pattern.')],
 'Potassium shift can alter muscle excitability', '**Periodic paralysis is a channel/excitability disorder in the appropriate setting.**', ['Measure potassium and ECG during attacks.','Exclude renal or gastrointestinal potassium loss and secondary causes.'], '**Correct potassium carefully.**', ['Redistribution can create rebound risk with excessive replacement.','Confirm the cause before choosing long-term prevention.'], [['Attack','Flaccid motor weakness'],['Potassium','Low during hypokalemic attacks'],['Evaluation','Shift versus true depletion']], [PP], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An adult with episodic hypokalemic paralysis also has weight loss, tremor and tachycardia. Which test is especially important?', 'Thyroid function testing for thyrotoxic periodic paralysis',[
 ('AChR antibodies as the only initial test','The systemic and potassium findings require thyroid assessment.'),('No further tests because all periodic paralysis is familial','Secondary thyrotoxic disease is important.'),('A normal family history excludes a potassium-shift syndrome','Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis is not dependent on such a history.')],
 'The endocrine cause changes definitive prevention', '**Thyrotoxicosis can promote a major intracellular potassium shift.**', ['The patient may have subtle rather than dramatic thyroid symptoms.','ECG monitoring and careful replacement are important.'], '**Restore euthyroidism and address attack triggers.**', ['Nonselective beta blockade may help selected patients without contraindications.','Avoid excessive potassium dosing because total-body depletion may be limited.'], [['Clue','Thyrotoxic symptoms'],['Mechanism','Redistribution'],['Definitive prevention','Treat thyrotoxicosis']], [PP], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Severe muscle pain follows exertion; CK is markedly raised and urine is heme-positive with few red cells. What diagnosis is most concerning?', 'Rhabdomyolysis with possible myoglobinuria',[
 ('The dipstick proves glomerulonephritis','Heme positivity can reflect pigment rather than red cells.'),('Normal urine color would exclude rhabdomyolysis','Myoglobin can clear rapidly and the classic triad is not universal.'),('A mild isolated muscle cramp requiring no renal assessment','Marked muscle injury warrants electrolyte and kidney evaluation.')],
 'Muscle breakdown can become a systemic emergency', '**Rhabdomyolysis releases CK, potassium and myoglobin from injured muscle.**', ['AKI, hyperkalemia and compartment syndrome are important complications.','Urine dipstick is not a sufficiently sensitive exclusion test.'], '**Assess severity and the trigger.**', ['Measure serial kidney function and electrolytes.','Review heat, drugs, seizures, trauma and metabolic predisposition.'], [['CK','Muscle injury marker'],['Heme-positive/few RBCs','Possible pigment'],['Complications','Potassium and kidney injury']], [RH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which initial management principle is appropriate in significant rhabdomyolysis?', 'Treat the cause, give goal-directed isotonic crystalloid when appropriate and monitor potassium, kidney function and volume status',[
 ('Routine bicarbonate and mannitol for every patient to prevent AKI','Evidence does not support routine prophylactic use.'),('Continue unlimited fluids despite anuria and pulmonary edema','Fluid overload requires reassessment.'),('Delay ECG evaluation until CK normalizes','Hyperkalemia can create immediate arrhythmic risk.')],
 'Fluid therapy needs a physiological target', '**Early appropriate crystalloid can support renal perfusion while avoiding overload.**', ['Adjust to urine output, comorbidity and clinical response.','Treat dangerous hyperkalemia promptly.'], '**Use dialysis for standard clinical indications.**', ['Do not prescribe it solely from a CK number.','Assess compartment syndrome and ongoing muscle injury.'], [['Fluids','Goal-directed'],['Bicarbonate/mannitol','Not routine AKI prophylaxis'],['Dialysis','Standard indications']], [RH])
q('Proximal weakness, cramps, elevated CK and delayed relaxation of ankle reflexes occur with cold intolerance. Which cause should be assessed?', 'Hypothyroid myopathy',[
 ('Pure MG as the only explanation','Delayed reflex relaxation and systemic features point to thyroid disease.'),('A normal CK is required for hypothyroidism','CK can be elevated.'),('Autoimmune inflammatory myositis is proven by enzyme elevation','Endocrine and toxic causes can produce the same laboratory clue.')],
 'An endocrine disorder can mimic inflammatory muscle disease', '**Hypothyroidism can cause weakness, myalgia and CK elevation.**', ['Reflex relaxation may be delayed.','Carpal tunnel neuropathy can coexist.'], '**Check thyroid function and treat the cause.**', ['Improvement may take time after replacement.','Persistent atypical weakness warrants further evaluation.'], [['Systemic clue','Cold intolerance and other thyroid features'],['Reflex','Delayed relaxation'],['Treatment','Thyroid replacement when indicated']], [TH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Ptosis and chronic external ophthalmoplegia occur with proximal weakness, hearing loss and diabetes. Which category is especially important?', 'A mitochondrial disorder',[
 ('A single ocular motor nerve lesion','This does not explain bilateral chronic ophthalmoplegia and multisystem involvement.'),('Pure uncomplicated MG as the only possible cause','The fixed multisystem pattern broadens the differential.'),('Typical carpal tunnel syndrome','This cannot explain the phenotype.')],
 'Energy-dependent organs can share one cause', '**Mitochondrial disorders can involve muscle, eye movements, hearing, endocrine and cardiac systems.**', ['Disease can arise from mitochondrial or nuclear genes.','Maternal inheritance applies to mitochondrial DNA mechanisms, not every mitochondrial disorder.'], '**Use phenotype-informed genetic and metabolic assessment.**', ['Consider cardiac conduction and respiratory risks.','A single normal lactate does not exclude every disorder.'], [['Phenotype','Multisystem energy failure'],['Genetics','Mitochondrial or nuclear'],['Assessment','Organ-specific risks']], [MI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which needle-EMG pattern generally supports a myopathic process?', 'Short-duration low-amplitude motor units with early recruitment in the appropriate clinical context',[
 ('Large long-duration units with reduced recruitment as the typical pattern','This more often suggests chronic neurogenic remodeling.'),('An isolated sensory conduction block','This localizes to a different component.'),('A marked postexercise motor increment as the defining finding','This suggests presynaptic transmission dysfunction.')],
 'Motor-unit morphology reflects the surviving muscle fibers', '**Myopathy can reduce the contribution of fibers within a motor unit.**', ['Irritable spontaneous activity is not unique to autoimmune myositis.','Some chronic or mixed disorders produce less classic patterns.'], '**Interpret physiology with clinical and laboratory evidence.**', ['EMG helps localize but does not always identify the exact cause.','Avoid biopsy of a recently needled site when it would confound interpretation.'], [['Myopathic','Short/small, early recruitment'],['Neurogenic','Often large/long, reduced recruitment'],['Cause','Requires further integration']], [BS])
q('What is the best principle for selecting muscle biopsy in suspected myopathy?', 'Choose an informative moderately affected muscle using clinical and imaging findings, avoiding end-stage or recently traumatized tissue where possible',[
 ('Always sample the weakest completely replaced muscle','End-stage tissue may yield nonspecific damage.'),('Biopsy every asymptomatic person with one postexercise CK elevation','Transient elevation requires context before invasive testing.'),('Any negative specimen excludes all genetic and patchy muscle disorders','Sampling and disease heterogeneity limit sensitivity.')],
 'Sampling determines diagnostic yield', '**Biopsy can distinguish inflammatory, necrotizing, dystrophic and metabolic patterns when appropriately selected.**', ['Some genetic diagnoses can be established without biopsy.','Coordinate tissue handling with the diagnostic laboratory.'], '**Define the question before sampling.**', ['Use the phenotype, antibodies and molecular tests to guide need.','A nondiagnostic result should trigger reassessment rather than automatic exclusion.'], [['Site','Moderately affected informative tissue'],['Technique','Appropriate handling'],['Interpretation','Clinical and molecular context']], [BS,DU,MI])

